mination. If you go in there and voice the first version—the honest unease—you might still not feel great, but you're not going to get yourself into trouble. And if you can translate that unease into specific, physical requests (good exterior lighting so elderly neighbors feel safe at night, a traffic plan for Friday prayers so the streets aren't clogged), you might actually make the situation better for everyone, yourself included. I'm not asking you to change your private beliefs. I'm asking you to decide what you want the outcome of that meeting to be. If it's just to speak your mind, do it. If it's to make your street safer in concrete ways, you have to use the tools the council can actually work with.
