23:44:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It's hard to argue with wanting to protect your neighbors. That instinct isn't wrong. It comes from a decent place. You're trying to be the person who sees trouble before it arrives, not the one who shrugs while something preventable unfolds. I understand why you frame it that way. Here's the only caution I'd offer, and it's not about politics or prejudice. It's about what actually makes neighborhoods safer in practice. If you're constantly on alert for danger from a specific group, you'll notice every small thing that confirms the threat and miss everything that contradicts it. A loud argument from a house becomes a sign of cultural dysfunction. A quiet family that keeps to themselves becomes unfriendly rather than just private. A traffic jam on Fridays becomes chaos instead of just a logistical problem to solve. You end up mistaking ordinary friction for existential danger. And a neighborhood that's constantly scanning for threats from its own residents doesn't become safer. It becomes brittle, suspicious, and divided. That's not vigilance. It's self-fulfilling anxiety. Real vigilance—the kind that actually catches problems early—requires being able to tell the difference between a real threat and a false alarm. And you can't do that if you've already decided, in advance, that the people moving in are likely to be trouble. You'll cry wolf too often, and when something genuinely dangerous does arise, you won't have the credibility or the relationships to deal with it effectively. The safest neighborhoods I've ever seen aren't the ones with the most suspicious neighbors watching from behind curtains. They're the ones where people know each other, talk to each other, and can tell the difference between a stranger who's lost and a stranger who's casing houses. That kind of knowledge only comes from openness, not from treating a whole community as a risk to be managed. So if you genuinely want to look out for your neighbors, the best thing you can do—after the meeting, after the zoning fights—is to actually get to know the people who move in. Learn their names. See how they treat their kids and their elders. Find out if they're the kind of people who'd call the cops if they saw someone breaking into your car. You might be surprised. Or you might confirm your fears. But at least you'll be operating from knowledge, not assumption. That's vigilance done right. The other way—pre-judging a whole faith—is just fear with a noble label.
